course: dictionary
lesson: 2375
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and cup is one of them. Context tells you which meaning is right: look at the words around it. In «Mr Hughes walks in with a cup of tea», a cup is something you drink from.
explain: The same word can mean a prize. «The girls did alright today» after a 5–0 cup final win means they were brilliant, and the cup final is a big match in a sport. Ask yourself: does this sentence make sense with a drink in mind, or with a prize?
explain: Cup can also be an amount. «Each cup holds 3 sweets» tells you how many fit in one group, and a recipe that asks for «2 cups of milk» is measuring. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. If it still makes sense, you have found the right one.
fact: A cup is a small container with a handle that you drink from.
fact: A cup is also an amount, such as «2 cups of milk» in a recipe.
fact: A cup is a prize in a sport, as in the cup final.
fact: To cup something means to hold it in curved hands.
fact: «Not my cup of tea» means not something I like.
exercise: «Mr Hughes walks in with a cup of tea.» What is a cup here? | *A small container you drink from | A prize in a sport | A measure used in baking
exercise: «Each cup holds 3 sweets.» What does cup mean in this sentence? | *A container, and the amount one group holds | A prize at the end of a match | To hold something in curved hands
exercise: The girls won the cup final 5–0. What is the cup here? | A drink you have with tea | *A prize in a big sport match | A measure of milk for a recipe
test: A recipe says «2 cups of milk». What is a cup here? | To hold milk in curved hands | A small container with a handle | *A measure of how much milk to use | A prize for the best baker
test: «Not my cup of tea» means what? | Holding something in curved hands | Something I drink every day | *Something I do not like | A prize I won
exam: Sam says, «I cup my hands to catch the rain.» What does cup mean? | A small container with a handle | A measure of rain in a bucket | A prize for the wettest day | *To hold something in curved hands
